{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 31, :comments ["<b>And you shall make the robe of the ephod entirely of tekhelet.</b> Since the robe atones for evil speech, therefore it was entirely of tekhelet, for tekhelet resembles the sea, and through the tekhelet one gazes upon the sea and from it he sees and so does: for concerning the sea it is said (Job 38:10–11), ״וָאֶשְׁבֹּר עָלָיו חֻקִּי וָאָשִׂים בְּרִיחַ וּדְלָתָיִם״ — and it says, ״עַד פֹּה תָבוֹא וְלֹא תוֹסִיף״ etc. And so the Holy One, blessed be He, gave to the tongue a wall and a bar, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Arakhin 15b): ״וְלֹא עוֹד, אֶלָּא שֶׁהִקַּפְתִּי לְךָ שְׁנֵי חוֹמוֹת, אַחַת שֶׁל עֶצֶם וְאַחַת שֶׁל בָּשָׂר״. Yet what avail, what added protection is there for a deceitful tongue? For when it goes out to the open, it speaks; it sends a sharpened arrow from its tongue to all four winds of the world, passing through bar and doors. And it would be fitting for a person to learn from the sea, which does not pass beyond its doors and bar and does not breach its boundary. And this is the reason for tekhelet, which resembles the sea: for when he gazes upon the tekhelet, he is reminded of the sea, which resembles it in color, and thereupon he contemplates the behavior of the sea, and from it he sees and so does." "And in parashat Shelach, with the help of HaShem, this same matter of the tekhelet of tzitzit will be explained in this connection, as it is concluded in the Yalkut of parashat Ha'azinu (912): Moses said: Gaze upon the heavens that I created to serve you — perhaps they changed their measure, etc. And so concerning the sea, as it is said (Jeremiah 5:22), ״הַאוֹתִי לֹא תִירָאוּ נְאֻם ה׳, אֲשֶׁר שַׂמְתִּי חוֹל גְּבוּל לַיָּם״ etc. And this is the reason for tekhelet for the remembrance of all the commandments of HaShem: for by gazing upon the tekhelet, which resembles the sea, he is reminded of the behavior of the sea, which does not breach its boundary and stands by the decree of HaShem, blessed be He — so he too shall do. And by gazing upon the sea, which resembles the firmament, he is also reminded of the behavior of the firmament, which does not change its measure, and from it he sees and so does, by an a fortiori argument. And by gazing upon the firmament, which resembles the Throne of Glory, it appears before his eyes as the place of his hewing, and he yearns to return and to cleave there, for the soul longs for the place where its tent was at first. And see further below in parashat Shelach (Numbers 15:38), for there is its place. But here the principal reason for tekhelet is to learn from the sea alone, as stated. Also, we may say that there is benefit as well in gazing upon the heavens and upon the Throne of Glory, for by this means one diminishes speech, as it is written (Ecclesiastes 5:1), ״כִּי הָאֱלֹהִים בַּשָּׁמַיִם וְאַתָּה עַל הָאָרֶץ, עַל כֵּן יִהְיוּ דְבָרֶיךָ מְעַטִּים״." "And concerning the matter of the enclosure by the two walls, it says, ״וְהָיָה פִי רֹאשׁוֹ בְּתוֹכוֹ שָׂפָה יִהְיֶה לְפִיו״ (Exodus 28:32) — for so must the tongue also be: the opening of its head is within it, inside the wall nearest to it, which is of bone; and corresponding to the wall of flesh, namely the lips, it says, ״שָׂפָה יִהְיֶה לְפִיו לֹא יִקָּרֵעַ״ — so that the two walls not be torn through together. And it does not say of it ״מַעֲשֵׂה חֹשֵׁב״, for the sin of the tongue is in speech specifically, not in thought. And upon it were pomegranates and bells that give forth sound, to atone for the tongue within the mouth — like the clapper within the bellows. And the rectification for the tongue is that he no longer profane his word, and speak only in words of holiness, as it says, ״וְנִשְׁמַע קוֹלוֹ בְּבֹאוֹ אֶל הַקֹּדֶשׁ וְלֹא יָמוּת״ (Exodus 28:35). For evil speech kills three (Arakhin 15b) — by inference, one who guards himself against it shall not die, as in the story of that peddler who used to announce, ״מַאן בָּעֵי לְמִזְבַּן סַם חַיֵּי״ etc., in the manner that will be explained in parashat Metzora with the help of HaShem."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:24:19.816943514Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}